welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is tonya sanchez an assistant professor  of political science at yale university  her teaching and research focus on  international cooperation and  international law in particular she  studies how international law and  organisations shape international  security and human rights policy she is  currently working on a book on the  domestic political factors that affect  treaty compliance today we talk with  Professor Sanchez about her supply-side  theory of treaty implementation and  compliance welcome professor Sanchez  thank you for having me let us talk  about your theory give us an overview so  the piece that I'm working on right now  has to look at domestic factors that  affect whether countries comply with  human rights agreements particularly  because of a political scientist the  politics that surround treaty  ratification and treaty implementation  and by that I mean how countries  actually take a treaty and make it part  of their domestic law outcome  institutions and I there's a lot of  research and political science in this  area but a little bit has looked at the  role that NGOs or other groups and civil  society have pushed States to adopt  treaties and implement them but not a  lot of work has looked at what i call  the supply side which is the government  mainly how legislators and executives go  about actually passing the loss and  putting them into the books so that  states can be in compliance with  international agreements okay what led  you to this work and how are you doing  the research so it started as part of my  doctoral work I was looking at trying to  explain why some countries seem to  undertake some international treaties  and do fairly well in complying but  others don't so it's sort of a puzzle  that I sought out to explore and one  that a lot of other scholars have  looked into and I started to look more  at the politics surrounding that and the  way I went about the research was by  first starting to look at what states  have actually done after they've  ratified agreements which you think that  would be a pretty obvious place to start  but there was not a lot of data so I  started by actually collecting a lot of  data going through each country's penal  codes and also the records of how  legislators debated different bills to  implement treaties into their domestic  legal system so I started by collecting  a lot of data and subsequently doing  some statistical analysis using that  data okay let's talk specifics what are  some of the countries that you could  cite for instance that may have enacted  a treaty a little bit differently than  perhaps a different another country are  there any correlations there are  differences that you could speak to oh  there's there's once you start looking  there wide differences and this is  what's interesting about the project so  when I started looking at their penal  codes there they all start all the  country start with the same treaty and  in one of the treaties i was looking was  a convention against torture okay and  they all started they all ratify the  treaty and so you would think that they  all of their loss would pretty much  mirror what the treaty said criminalized  torture put some sanctions and I started  by looking at Latin America because the  region is very uniform and lots of  variables that allow me to control for  other circumstances they if you look at  the definitions they define torture very  differently from country to country for  instance countries like Paraguay will  make a requisite that you prove intent  when you're trying to convict somebody  of the crime of torture which is not  part of the treaty they just sort of  threw that in there the sanctions vary  widely so you have a country like  Argentina that will punish torture from  eight to twenty years Wow but Paraguay  will be like two months to six months  so two months six months 8 to 20 years  big discrepancies so I thought why why  are some countries being very strong  implementers really following the treaty  putting strong sanctions why do we have  other countries that have you know  definitions that don't conform to the  treaty and sanctions that are you know  very weak and might not get us to and  how does your theory explain these  differences these variances politics  okay I'm the theory sort of has a lot to  do with who is in power not just at the  time that the treaty was ratified but  who is in power when they actually have  to put the loss in the books the  preferences of the party in power but  not just what their preferences are but  also who their political opposition is  and whether they can actually get those  laws through the legislative process as  you know getting a bill you know past is  very complicated so often miracle when  it happened yes it is so now I don't  only looked at how the politics affected  you know amendments of bills but the  time that it took some countries take  after they ratified 32 years by the to  get the law some countries like Uruguay  20 years why did it take 20 years well  they couldn't agree and then the bill  was killed in committee and then it just  didn't get picked up again until they  had to change the power another party  came in they retook the bill finally  passed it through I had more support  they had a majority in the legislature  so they didn't have to deal with a lot  of them the hassles that it takes for a  bill to get through so I look at that  process and explain how you know when  they disagree about what should what  they want domestically to happen it  stalls the process and so we have a lot  of states that get stuck in that process  it's a long and complicated process so  how does your supply side theory differ  from current thinking it doesn't  necessarily differ it sort of  supplements a side that has hadn't been  studied a lot of other theories which I  call the demand side explain  how different people within the polity  demand that the state undertake these  agreements and implement them and so  that research has looked a lot of how  NGOs civil society activists lawyers etc  push the government to actually get  those laws in the books so they're  looking sort of a one piece of the  puzzle and I'm looking at another piece  of the puzzle but they going to  complement each other because a lot of  the states don't really get these bills  going until there is that you know  demand from the people but then I go in  and say but it's that's not enough the  demand is not enough you still have to  have enough support within the  legislature to get those bills going and  actually get it in the books and then  subsequent to the law being passed to  actually fulfill the law train your  police I locate fonts you know develop  the policies that really get compliance  with the treaties and that takes a lot  of a lot of work do you ever think that  there will be a time when treaties can  be enacted on an equal level where one  country does it the same as another  country it seems like politics will  always have some role to play in that  process I don't think that they'll ever  be identical and but I think that's a  good thing because not all countries are  identical so you need to allow for some  countries to sort of you know tailor it  for their own individual situation their  own legal systems vary widely so what  makes you know a law that makes a lot of  sense in one country and another country  wouldn't work particularly well but you  what you don't want is huge  discrepancies so you need to narrow that  gap of what's what's acceptable  variation and I think that part of how  you achieve that is by writing better  treaties the treaties leave up a lot of  room for interpretation you know they'll  say something like don't torture torture  bad don't do it but then they leave a  lot of the rest up to the states to fill  in the blanks and so if we were able to  write better treaties and maybe also at  the international level give  implementation assistance to  countries so they have a better idea of  what the international community expects  when they undertake these treaties that  we would see the less variation okay and  in terms of any conclusions that you  have in terms of policy and and how best  you know to move forward with enacting  treaties what would you suggest I think  from the international community is give  a lot more assistance to States both  legal technical assistance so to  legislators to give them sample  legislation of what we expect you to  look at a lot of the times states have  to draft their own from scratch and this  is why we see a lot of variation but in  other treaties we have seen that  international bodies like the UN will  present to them a sample legislation  that often states just identically take  it and try to pass that through also  make it requirement that states actually  pass a law within a time period and not  allow states to you know go on 20 years  or so without actually doing something  so following up with the states and  giving them assistance helping them what  is it that's telling the process  sometimes there's not much you can be  done because of its politics you just  have to wait till then till you know  there's a configuration that comes in or  try to convince them and that that's  where the civil society part is  important to try to persuade the  legislators and the people that this is  a desirable bill and that it should be  passed so there's a lot of things that  can be done depending on whether you  want to operate domestically or whether  you want to operate at it from an  international level so we think that o  scholars think that there's a lot of  capacity building that could help  countries giving them the right tools to  achieve the treaty goals right okay very  good thank you so much for being here  with us today and sharing some of your  work great thank you for having me be  sure to join us again for another  episode of the Macmillan report made  possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International  area studies at Yale  you
 

